{%- comment -%}
  Extension point provided by al_folio_core's _layouts/bib.liquid:

      {% capture hook_exists %}{% file_exists _includes/hook/bib.liquid %}{% endcapture %}
      {% if hook_exists == 'true' %}{% include hook/bib.liquid %}{% endif %}

  It runs once per bibliography entry, with `entry` in scope, and whatever it outputs is
  placed just above the journal/date line. Using the hook rather than overriding the
  411-line theme layout means theme upgrades keep working.

  What it renders, and why each earns a badge rather than plain text:

    * AUTHOR ROLE (`authorship`) — first, co-first, corresponding, co-author. This is what
      a reader of an academic publication list is actually trying to establish, and it is
      not recoverable by eye from a list of seventeen names. Every entry carries one, so
      "no badge" never has to be interpreted.
    * PREPRINT (`keywords = {preprint}`) — the opposite concern: a non-peer-reviewed item
      must never read as a journal publication on a public page.

  Both are semantic values set in the bibliography source of truth, deliberately kept out
  of the free-text `note` field so that no renderer has to string-match prose that might
  later be reworded. This file only decides how they look; the source decides what is
  true. Colours come from al-folio's theme custom properties, so the badges follow
  light/dark mode without any extra stylesheet.

  ── Matching ──
  Roles combine: `first,co-first` means listed first AND credited as shared first. Split
  on the comma and test exact tokens rather than using `contains` on the whole string —
  "co-first" contains "first", so a substring test would badge every shared-first paper as
  plain first authorship too.

  Where both apply, only "Co-first author" is shown. Printing "First author · Co-first
  author" side by side says the same thing twice and reads as a contradiction.

  `authorship` is listed in `filtered_bibtex_keywords` in _config.yml, which stops the
  theme from also dumping it as a raw field on the page.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign flag_style = "display:inline-block;margin:0 0.35rem 0.25rem 0;padding:0.1rem 0.5rem;border-radius:0.2rem;font-size:0.75rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.02em;line-height:1.5;" -%}
{%- assign roles = entry.authorship | replace: " ", "" | split: "," -%}

{%- comment -%}
  No per-entry figure here, deliberately.

  Figures were rendered inside each entry for a while. Five pictures scattered down a
  twenty-five item list broke the list's rhythm without ever showing the work as a whole
  — the reader met them one at a time, out of context. They are now composed into a
  single panel on the front page (assets/img/selected_work.png, built by
  bin/make-figure-panel.py), which is where a visitor actually looks for a sense of the
  research.

  `preview` fields stay in the bibliography: they are what the panel builder reads to
  know which figure belongs to which paper, and what its caption should say.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if entry.keywords contains "preprint" -%}
  <span
    style="{{ flag_style }}color:var(--global-warning-block-title);border:1px solid var(--global-warning-block);background:var(--global-warning-block-bg);"
    title="Not peer reviewed. This is a preprint, not a journal publication."
    >Preprint &middot; not peer reviewed</span
  >
{%- endif -%}

{%- if roles contains "co-first" -%}
  <span
    style="{{ flag_style }}color:var(--global-theme-color);border:1px solid var(--global-theme-color);background:transparent;"
    title="Shared first authorship on this paper."
    >Co-first author</span
  >
{%- elsif roles contains "first" -%}
  <span
    style="{{ flag_style }}color:var(--global-theme-color);border:1px solid var(--global-theme-color);background:transparent;"
    title="First-listed author on this paper."
    >First author</span
  >
{%- endif -%}

{%- if roles contains "corresponding" -%}
  <span
    style="{{ flag_style }}color:var(--global-card-bg-color);border:1px solid var(--global-theme-color);background:var(--global-theme-color);"
    title="Corresponding author on this paper."
    >Corresponding author</span
  >
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Co-authorship is stated, not advertised: a lighter border and muted text. It is the
  answer to "what was the role here", not a claim to make loudly, and twelve shouting
  badges would drown out the eight that matter.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if roles contains "co-author" -%}
  <span
    style="{{ flag_style }}font-weight:500;color:var(--global-text-color-light);border:1px solid var(--global-divider-color);background:transparent;"
    title="Contributing author on this paper."
    >Co-author</span
  >
{%- endif -%}
